---
import destinations from '../data/destinations.json';
import type { DestinationClimate } from '../data/climate';

const example = (destinations as DestinationClimate[])[0];
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width" />
    <meta name="description" content="Methodology and data attribution for Where Is It Sunny historical climate averages." />
    <title>Methodology | Where Is It Sunny?</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <main>
      <p><a href="/">← Find destinations</a></p>
      <h1>Methodology and data attribution</h1>

      <section aria-labelledby="source">
        <h2 id="source">Source and period</h2>
        <p>
          Climate data is generated at build time from the <a href={example.source.documentationUrl}>NASA POWER Climatology API</a>.
          The current dataset covers {example.climate.period.description}; it records the source metadata returned with each API request.
        </p>
        <p>
          Data attribution: <a href="https://power.larc.nasa.gov/">NASA POWER</a>. NASA's <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/earth-science/earth-science-data/data-information-policy">Earth-science data policy</a> supports full and open sharing, while its <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/nasa-brand-center/images-and-media/">public-use guidance</a> says NASA should be acknowledged and its name must not imply endorsement. This site
          uses a factual source attribution and no NASA logos. Recheck the applicable terms before any commercial launch.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="metrics">
        <h2 id="metrics">Metrics</h2>
        <dl>
          <dt>Mean temperature</dt>
          <dd>{example.source.parameters.temperature.code}: {example.source.parameters.temperature.name}, shown in {example.source.parameters.temperature.unit}.</dd>
          <dt>Average daily high</dt>
          <dd>{example.source.parameters.averageDailyMaximumTemperature.code}: {example.source.parameters.averageDailyMaximumTemperature.name}, shown in {example.source.parameters.averageDailyMaximumTemperature.unit}. NASA POWER's similarly named <code>T2M_MAX</code> is a monthly extreme, so this site does not use it as an average daily high.</dd>
          <dt>Rainfall</dt>
          <dd>{example.source.parameters.precipitation.code}: {example.source.parameters.precipitation.name}. NASA POWER supplies mm/day; this site multiplies it by calendar days in the month to show an estimated monthly amount. It is not rainy-day count.</dd>
          <dt>Solar radiation</dt>
          <dd>{example.source.parameters.solarRadiation.code}: {example.source.parameters.solarRadiation.name}, shown in {example.source.parameters.solarRadiation.unit}. It is not hours of sunshine.</dd>
        </dl>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="limits">
        <h2 id="limits">Limits</h2>
        <p>Each destination is a representative API point, not a claim about every neighbourhood, beach, mountain, or island microclimate. Historical climate averages describe past conditions; they cannot predict weather for a specific holiday.</p>
        <p>The generator rejects NASA POWER's reported fill value ({example.source.fillValue}) instead of replacing missing data with zero.</p>
      </section>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>

<style>
  :global(body) { color: #1b2630; font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0; }
  main { margin: 0 auto; max-width: 48rem; padding: 2rem 1rem 4rem; }
  dt { font-weight: 700; margin-top: 1rem; }
  dd { margin-left: 0; }
</style>
